        {[
          { q: "How long does it take to scale from 10 to 100 videos per month?", a: "Typically 3-6 months. The first 4-6 weeks are the foundation stage (10 videos), where you find product-content fit. Weeks 6-12 are the systemization stage (30 videos), where you build templates, onboard creators, and establish workflows. Months 3-6 are the scaling stage, where you ramp volume while refining quality control. Rushing this timeline usually leads to wasted budget and burnt-out teams." },
          { q: "How many creators do I need for 100 videos per month?", a: "Plan for 30-50 active creators. Each creator typically produces 2-4 videos per month at this volume. You also need a continuous recruiting pipeline adding 5-10 new creators monthly to offset the 10-20% natural churn rate. At OKAD, we source from a network of 700+ vetted creators to keep programs running without gaps." },
          { q: "What does it cost to produce 100 UGC videos per month?", a: "Costs vary by model. Canvas UGC programs achieve $15-$30 per video at volume ($1,500-$3,000/month in creator payments). Traditional UGC with individual creator fees runs $80-$200 per video ($8,000-$20,000/month). Management overhead, tooling, and QC add 20-30% on top. Working with an agency like OKAD often reduces total cost because the infrastructure is already built." },
          { q: "Can I scale UGC in-house or do I need an agency?", a: "You can scale in-house, but the infrastructure investment is significant: creator sourcing, onboarding systems, QC workflows, payment processing, and analytics tooling. Most brands find it efficient to partner with an agency for the first 3-6 months to build the playbook, then decide whether to bring production in-house or continue the partnership. We work both ways." },
          { q: "How do I maintain brand consistency with 30-50 creators?", a: "Brand consistency at scale comes from systems, not supervision. Build a brand guide with visual references, do/don't examples, and approved talking points. Bake these into your brief templates so every creator gets the same guardrails. Use the QC rubric to catch deviations. Auto-approve creators who consistently stay on-brand, and give detailed feedback to those who drift." },
        ].map((item) => (
